<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<rqml xmlns="https://rqml.org/schema/2.2.0"
      xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
      version="2.2.0"
      docId="DOC-MOTLEY"
      status="draft"
      xsi:schemaLocation="https://rqml.org/schema/2.2.0 https://rqml.org/schema/rqml-2.2.0.xsd">
  <meta>
    <title>Motley requirements</title>
    <system>motley</system>
    <summary>Motley requirements under development. The engineering requirements capture the portable-core and typed-result directions; draft hardware requirements address complete pin-state review and defined defaults. Product behavior and verification remain to be developed.</summary>
  </meta>
  <goals>
    <goal id="GOAL-MOT-PLATFORM" title="Matter device foundation" priority="must" status="draft">
      <statement>Establish a commissionable ESP32-C6 Matter-over-Thread device with accessible device and firmware information before adding sensing or control behavior.</statement>
      <rationale>The developer selected this as the next milestone after the minimal build and has Home Assistant and a working Thread border router available for tests.</rationale>
    </goal>
    <goal id="GOAL-MOT-PROCESS" title="Requirements-led incremental development" priority="must" status="draft">
      <statement>Guide maintained implementation with agreed requirements while allowing future requirements to remain visible as unfinished work.</statement>
      <rationale>The developer wants to favor requirements before implementation while learning where exceptions are useful, without requiring the entire backlog to be implemented.</rationale>
    </goal>
    <goal id="GOAL-MOT-BUILD" title="Buildable firmware foundation" priority="must" status="draft">
      <statement>Establish an executable firmware build before developing device functionality.</statement>
      <rationale>The developer requested a minimal do-nothing task to prove the build and defer functional design.</rationale>
    </goal>
    <goal id="GOAL-MOT-ENGINEERING" title="Engineering" priority="must" status="draft">
      <statement>Keep Motley's application behavior understandable and testable independently of the controller hardware and external integrations.</statement>
      <rationale>Host execution should allow development and verification of application behavior before hardware is available.</rationale>
    </goal>
    <goal id="GOAL-MOT-SENSING" title="Wired sensing" priority="must" status="draft">
      <statement>Provide remote temperature and humidity measurements using wired sensor heads in later application milestones.</statement>
      <rationale>Sensing remains future work after the Matter device foundation; its requirements can be recorded before implementation.</rationale>
    </goal>
    <goal id="GOAL-MOT-HARDWARE" title="Predictable hardware states" priority="must" status="draft">
      <statement>Make hardware behavior predictable when signal drivers are high impedance, supplies change state, or removable connections are absent.</statement>
      <rationale>The developer requested consideration of pull-up and pull-down needs for every component pin, including startup states of MCUs and other chips and disconnected low-voltage connectors or jumpers.</rationale>
    </goal>
    <goal id="GOAL-MOT-AC" title="Locally controlled and monitored AC outputs" priority="must" status="draft">
      <statement>Control compatible AC heaters and lamps locally, identify output behavior inconsistent with commands, and interrupt unintended power delivery.</statement>
      <rationale>The developer wants both lamp dimming and slow heater modulation, including identification of a shorted triac on each output with or without an attached load.</rationale>
    </goal>
    <goal id="GOAL-MOT-PERSISTENCE" title="Recoverable persistent state on every MCU" priority="must" status="draft">
      <statement>Retain a complete committed persistent state through interrupted updates, restarts and power cycles on every Motley MCU.</statement>
      <rationale>The developer requires double-buffered persistent storage with verification before changing the active pointer.</rationale>
    </goal>
  </goals>
  <requirements>
    <req id="MOT-PLAT-001" type="CR" title="Compatible pinned Matter firmware stack" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>Motley's ESP32-C6 Matter firmware SHALL use a compatible, pinned ESP-IDF, esp-matter, and connectedhomeip dependency set with explicit resolved dependency identities.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PLAT-001-01">
          <given>a developer preparing the documented Matter firmware toolchain</given>
          <when>the declared SDK and dependency inputs are inspected and resolved</when>
          <then>the compatible SDK revisions and target are explicit, dependency resolution is recorded, and the build does not silently select moving upstream branches</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PLAT-001-02">
          <given>a clean checkout and the documented prerequisites</given>
          <when>the Matter firmware build executes</when>
          <then>it uses the declared dependency set and records the resolved build inputs and artifacts through the existing provenance workflow</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-PLAT-004" type="FR" title="Commissionable Matter-over-Thread foundation" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>A supported ESP32-C6 controller SHALL be commissionable into Home Assistant as a Matter-over-Thread device, retain its commissioning state across ordinary power cycles, and provide an intentional documented reset procedure for recommissioning. The development interface SHALL provide commissioning-code output and deliberate factory reset through USB serial.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PLAT-004-01">
          <given>an uncommissioned controller, Home Assistant, and a working Thread border router</given>
          <when>the documented commissioning procedure is followed</when>
          <then>Home Assistant commissions the device onto the Thread network and can read its Matter device information without requiring a sensor or actuator to be attached</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PLAT-004-02">
          <given>a commissioned controller with readable device information</given>
          <when>it loses power and restarts with unchanged firmware and configuration</when>
          <then>it reconnects under the existing commissioning state and Home Assistant can read its information again without factory reset, recommissioning, or a duplicate device entry</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PLAT-004-03">
          <given>a commissioned controller</given>
          <when>the intentional documented factory-reset procedure is performed</when>
          <then>its previous commissioning state is cleared and it can be commissioned again; an ordinary reboot does not perform that reset</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PLAT-004-04">
          <given>the development controller connected through the documented USB serial interface</given>
          <when>the developer follows the setup and explicit factory-reset procedures</when>
          <then>the commissioning code is available through serial output and an explicit serial request can perform factory reset; physical setup/reset controls are not required for this development milestone</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-INFO-001" type="IR" title="Accessible device and firmware identity" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>The commissioned device SHALL expose declared product, hardware, stable device, and firmware identity through Matter Basic Information, with its firmware version available in Home Assistant's device information.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-INFO-001-01">
          <given>a commissioned device running an identified firmware build</given>
          <when>its Basic Information attributes and Home Assistant device information are inspected</when>
          <then>the declared vendor/product identity, hardware version, stable device identifier, numeric software version, and software version string are readable through their applicable standard attributes, and Home Assistant displays the firmware version string</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-INFO-001-02">
          <given>a build identity that fits the standard Matter software-version-string capacity</given>
          <when>that attribute is read through Matter and displayed by Home Assistant</when>
          <then>it matches the full commit hash and clean/dirty identity embedded in the running firmware and its build receipt rather than inheriting the shorter SDK label; constrained fields may abbreviate only as permitted by MOT-BUILD-002</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-INFO-001-03">
          <given>the same controller and firmware after an ordinary reboot</given>
          <when>device and firmware information are read again</when>
          <then>the stable device identifier remains unchanged and firmware identity still describes the running build</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-PROC-001" type="PR" title="Requirements before maintained implementation" status="approved" priority="should">
      <statement>Meaningful maintained implementation SHOULD follow agreed requirements specified before coding, with its implementation linked to those requirements and any exception recorded with its reason and scope.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PROC-001-01">
          <given>a proposed change to maintained behavior, constraints, or build/integration behavior</given>
          <when>the change is planned and reviewed</when>
          <then>the governing agreed requirements and affected obligations are identified before implementation, or an explicit scoped exception is recorded</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PROC-001-02">
          <given>helpers, tests, refactoring, or exploratory work</given>
          <when>the work is classified</when>
          <then>ordinary implementation details may support an existing requirement without a separate requirement per function or file; exceptions and temporary experiments are identified rather than silently promoted to maintained features</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PROC-001-03">
          <given>a meaningful implementation diff</given>
          <when>traceability is reviewed</when>
          <then>actual changed source and configuration are compared with the requirement mappings, including unlinked implementation that RQML cannot discover from the trace graph</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-PROC-002" type="PR" title="Backlog-tolerant requirements consistency checks" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>During incremental development, repository requirements checks SHALL permit unimplemented and unverified requirements while rejecting invalid specifications, missing or changed linked implementations, and implementation mapped to unapproved requirements.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PROC-002-01">
          <given>draft or approved requirements lacking implementation or verification</given>
          <when>the normal repository requirements checks execute</when>
          <then>those coverage gaps remain reportable through status and matrix without blocking the checks solely for incomplete coverage</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-PROC-002-02">
          <given>an invalid specification, missing/changed linked implementation, or implementation mapped to an unapproved requirement</given>
          <when>the corresponding normal consistency or approval check executes</when>
          <then>the check fails, independently of the nonblocking backlog policy</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-BUILD-002" type="DR" title="Firmware build identity and provenance" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>Each successful firmware build SHALL carry a versioned receipt identifying its actual source checkout, working-tree state, build inputs, environment, execution context, outputs, and verification results, and SHALL embed its full commit hash and clean/dirty state in the firmware. Full commit hashes SHALL remain present and accessible in firmware metadata and build receipts. Size-limited standard fields MAY abbreviate them when required.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-BUILD-002-01">
          <given>a local attached or detached checkout with staged, unstaged, or nonignored untracked changes</given>
          <when>build identity is collected before output generation</when>
          <then>the receipt records the full actual commit, committed tree, parents, attached branch or null, separate staged/unstaged/untracked indicators, and an actual-input fingerprint; only the designated build-output directory is excluded from cleanliness checks</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-BUILD-002-02">
          <given>a CI checkout with optional pull-request or merge-queue context</given>
          <when>source provenance is recorded</when>
          <then>actual checkout identity is distinct from event identity; checkout kind, checkout ref, PR number/URL, source and target repository/branch/commit, and inclusion of the exact recorded base commit are reported from available evidence, using null or unknown for unavailable information</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-BUILD-002-03">
          <given>a successful firmware build</given>
          <when>its receipt and application image are inspected</when>
          <then>the ELF and application image contain the full commit and clean/dirty state without truncation; displayed build identities and artifact names use the full commit, while size-limited standard fields may abbreviate it with the full identity remaining accessible; the receipt records UTC build time, target, SDK/container/compiler identity, effective build settings, configuration/input hashes, output names/sizes/hashes, check results, hardware-test status, and available CI event/workflow revision/run/attempt/job/URL</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-BUILD-002-04">
          <given>build inputs or checkout state changing during compilation, or existing outputs being reverified</given>
          <when>the build or verification check completes</when>
          <then>changed inputs or mismatched provenance cause failure, while successful reverification preserves the original receipt including its time and CI context</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-CI-001" type="PR" title="Firmware CI build check" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>CI SHALL build and verify firmware for every pull request and push to main using the documented project build check and declared toolchain.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-001-01">
          <given>a pull request or push to main</given>
          <when>CI executes</when>
          <then>a separate firmware job uses a fresh output directory without connected hardware and invokes the same build/verification check available locally</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-001-02">
          <given>a compilation or verification failure</given>
          <when>the firmware job and aggregate CI check complete</when>
          <then>the firmware job fails and the aggregate check cannot pass</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-CI-002" type="PR" title="Retained firmware artifacts" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>CI SHALL retain a downloadable, identified bundle of each successfully verified firmware build and retain available failed-build diagnostics separately.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-002-01">
          <given>a successful firmware build and verification</given>
          <when>CI publishes its artifact</when>
          <then>the bundle contains the application ELF/image, bootloader, partition table, flashing metadata with matching relative paths, linker map, receipt, verification and size reports; its name includes target, full commit hash, clean/dirty state, checkout context, run ID and attempt</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-002-02">
          <given>missing required bundle files or a failed artifact upload</given>
          <when>publication runs</when>
          <then>the firmware job fails rather than reporting successful publication</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-002-03">
          <given>a failed build with available diagnostics</given>
          <when>failure artifacts are retained</when>
          <then>they are labelled as failed diagnostics and are separate from verified firmware bundles</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-CI-003" type="PR" title="Downloadable human-readable requirements report" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>Every pull-request and main-push CI run SHALL generate a human-readable Markdown report from its checked-out RQML specification and retain it as a downloadable artifact associated with that commit and run.</statement>
      <rationale>The developer requested building the standard RQML overview report and retaining it in CI, and explicitly identified this as a CI requirement.</rationale>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-003-01">
          <given>a valid checked-out requirements.rqml on a pull request or push to main</given><when>the CI report job runs</when><then>the pinned RQML overview command generates a nonempty Markdown report containing requirement identifiers, titles, lifecycle statuses, statements and acceptance criteria, including draft requirements</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-003-02">
          <given>a generated requirements report</given><when>CI publishes it</when><then>it is downloadable as an artifact named with its checked-out commit, run identifier and attempt; report generation and upload do not depend on the firmware job succeeding</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-003-03">
          <given>report generation failure, a missing or empty report, or upload failure</given><when>CI completes</when><then>the requirements-report job fails and the aggregate check cannot pass</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-CI-004" type="PR" title="Reproducible committed board SVGs" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>Motley SHALL version its per-board SVG review images in a dedicated generated directory as derivatives of the native KiCad boards. Local pre-commit SHALL regenerate missing or stale expected images, while CI SHALL reject out-of-date derivatives using a reproducible read-only export check.</statement>
      <rationale>The developer explicitly requested keeping the SVGs committed, normalizing their timestamps, checking freshness in pre-commit and CI, and clearly separating generated derivatives from native sources.</rationale>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-004-01">
          <given>the maintained board views and their native KiCad sources</given><when>the documented regeneration command runs</when><then>it generates the configured coloured SVG views in a dedicated versioned generated directory whose documentation identifies their source and regeneration command</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-004-02">
          <given>unchanged native inputs, declared export settings and the pinned KiCad version</given><when>images are regenerated independently</when><then>their bytes are identical without relying on the previous images, local KiCad preferences, export time or output path; volatile timestamp metadata is normalized without changing plotted geometry</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-004-03">
          <given>a missing, stale or altered expected SVG in the generated image directory</given><when>the local pre-commit formatter executes</when><then>it regenerates that derivative without changing native CAD and fails the hook when files were created or updated so the developer can review and stage them before retrying; a new or recreated derivative remains blocking until staged, matching files remain untouched and unexpected SVGs still fail explicitly</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-004-04">
          <given>matching committed derivatives or an unavailable, incompatible or failing exporter</given><when>the freshness check executes</when><then>matching images pass, and exporter failures fail explicitly rather than silently accepting the existing files</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-CI-004-05">
          <given>a missing, stale, altered or unexpected SVG in the generated image directory</given><when>CI executes its freshness check</when><then>it fails with actionable diagnostics without rewriting native sources or generated images, and CI cannot pass the repository checks while the failure remains</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-BUILD-001" type="FR" title="Minimal ESP32-C6 firmware build" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>Motley SHALL provide a C++ ESP32-C6 firmware target with a documented build using a version-pinned ESP-IDF environment. Its application behavior SHALL follow the approved milestone requirements.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-BUILD-001-01">
          <given>a checkout and the documented build prerequisites without connected hardware</given>
          <when>the firmware build check runs in a clean output directory</when>
          <then>compilation and linking succeed and produce nonempty ESP32-C6 application ELF/image, bootloader, and partition-table artifacts using the declared SDK environment</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-BUILD-001-02">
          <given>the minimal firmware entry point and effective build settings</given>
          <when>the source and build evidence are inspected</when>
          <then>owned C++ sources use the selected C++23 mode with exceptions and RTTI disabled; application startup follows the approved milestone requirements</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-BUILD-001-03">
          <given>the documented build check</given>
          <when>artifact verification runs after compilation</when>
          <then>it checks the target metadata, effective owned-source compiler settings, ELF and image artifacts, and reports failures without claiming that the firmware has booted on hardware</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-ENG-004" type="NFR" title="Host-testable portable C++ core" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Motley's application core SHALL be portable C++ that can be built and tested on a development host without ESP32 hardware or the ESP-IDF, FreeRTOS, and Matter SDK runtimes.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-004-01">
          <given>a checkout with the documented host compiler, build tools, and host-test dependencies</given>
          <when>the host core test target is built and executed</when>
          <then>the core and its tests compile and run without installing the firmware SDKs, connecting controller hardware, or starting a Matter runtime</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-004-02">
          <given>application-core behavior included in the firmware</given>
          <when>the firmware and host test targets are inspected</when>
          <then>both targets build the same core implementation rather than separate copies of its behavior</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-ENG-005" type="CR" title="Explicit core environment boundary" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Clock access, sensor I/O, persistent-storage I/O, and Matter I/O SHALL execute outside the application core in adapters. The core SHALL receive environmental observations and operation results as explicit inputs and expose requested external work as explicit outputs.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-005-01">
          <given>core behavior that depends on time or an external operation</given>
          <when>that behavior is exercised in a host test</when>
          <then>the test supplies time and operation results explicitly and inspects requested work without executing device, storage, or network I/O from the core</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-005-02">
          <given>the application core and its build dependencies</given>
          <when>the environment boundary is reviewed</when>
          <then>platform clock, sensor-driver, storage, and Matter APIs are confined to adapters and are not called by the core</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-ENG-006" type="CR" title="Explicit typed results for recoverable failures" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>At application-owned C++ function boundaries, recoverable operational failures SHALL be represented by explicitly typed success/error results using std::expected&lt;T, E&gt; or std::expected&lt;void, E&gt;. These result-returning functions SHALL be marked [[nodiscard]], and an unacknowledged discarded result SHALL be diagnosed as a build error.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-006-01">
          <given>an application-owned operation that can report a recoverable failure</given>
          <when>its API and success/failure paths are reviewed and tested</when>
          <then>the function returns std::expected with a typed error representation and callers handle or propagate the failure without using exceptions for routine operational control flow</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-006-02">
          <given>a fallible application-owned function declaration</given>
          <when>a compile check calls it and discards its result without explicit acknowledgement</when>
          <then>the configured owned-code build rejects that call because the function is [[nodiscard]]</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-006-03">
          <given>a recoverable operation without a success payload</given>
          <when>its application success/failure paths are exercised</when>
          <then>callers distinguish successful completion from the operation's typed failure through std::expected&lt;void, E&gt; without inventing a success sentinel value</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-ENG-007" type="CR" title="Exceptions-disabled production C++" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Motley-owned production C++ code SHALL be compiled with C++ exception handling disabled. Production dependencies SHALL be configured and used so that they do not propagate C++ exceptions across application boundaries.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-007-01">
          <given>the production firmware build and a host build of the same core</given>
          <when>the owned production sources' effective compiler settings are inspected and the targets are built</when>
          <then>C++ exception handling is disabled for those sources in both builds</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-007-02">
          <given>a production dependency being introduced or upgraded</given>
          <when>its configuration and used API failure paths are reviewed</when>
          <then>its exception requirements and failure behavior are recorded, and its selected configuration and use do not propagate exceptions into application code</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-007-03">
          <given>a host test framework that uses exceptions internally</given>
          <when>it runs tests of the production core</when>
          <then>the core remains compiled with exception handling disabled and test-framework exceptions do not unwind through the core</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-ENG-008" type="CR" title="Bounded application memory" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Motley-owned production code SHALL have explicit bounds for buffers, collections, queues, and task stacks. It SHOULD prefer static storage or bounded stack storage. Dynamic allocation SHALL be restricted to justified uses with documented capacity bounds and lifetimes.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-008-01">
          <given>a supported application configuration</given>
          <when>its memory design and selected capacities are reviewed</when>
          <then>owned buffers, collections, queues, and task stacks have explicit upper bounds, and each owned dynamic-allocation use has a stated reason, capacity bound, and lifetime</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-008-02">
          <given>an application capacity limit and input at or beyond that limit</given>
          <when>the relevant input or work is processed</when>
          <then>the implementation respects the declared bound instead of growing memory use without limit, and ordinary capacity rejection follows the typed-result policy where applicable</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-008-03">
          <given>the selected firmware configuration and supported operating workload</given>
          <when>target memory usage is evaluated</when>
          <then>the memory assessment accounts for static storage, task stacks, owned dynamic allocations, and SDK memory use, and records observed usage against the selected budgets</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-ENG-009" type="FR" title="Fatal handling of detected memory faults" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Detected memory-integrity faults and unexpected memory exhaustion SHALL enter a fatal-fault path that stops normal application execution. They SHALL NOT be treated as routine recoverable operation failures.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-009-01">
          <given>a detected memory-integrity fault or unexpected memory exhaustion</given>
          <when>the selected fault-injection procedure exercises its handling</when>
          <then>the fatal-fault path is entered and normal application execution does not continue by returning an ordinary recoverable result</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-009-02">
          <given>input that exceeds a declared application capacity and can be rejected before a memory fault</given>
          <when>that input is validated</when>
          <then>the ordinary validation/capacity error remains distinguishable from an actual memory-integrity fault or unexpected memory exhaustion</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-ENG-010" type="FR" title="Per-MCU watchdog supervision and response" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Every Motley MCU SHALL have watchdog supervision with an explicit healthy-progress signal, a bounded expiry interval and a defined fault response that operates when the supervised application cannot execute normally.</statement>
      <notes>This includes the main ESP, every output MCU and future MCUs. A hardware internal watchdog is acceptable when its clock, refresh path and response meet the declared failure model; a separate external watchdog pin or component is not prescribed. Healthy progress is evidence of the supervised work, not merely a periodic interrupt or incoming bus traffic. The selected physical or software feed/heartbeat signal and reset/inhibit path require explicit documentation.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-010-01">
          <given>the complete MCU inventory and supported operating modes</given><when>watchdog coverage is reviewed</when><then>each MCU has a documented watchdog mechanism, counter/clock dependence, supervised critical work, feed/heartbeat source and progress conditions, maximum expiry/response time, activation point, reset or inhibition path and restart policy</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-010-02">
          <given>an MCU with each supervised critical task stalled or deadlocked in turn, including cases where interrupts or other tasks continue</given><when>healthy progress stops</when><then>unrelated interrupt activity, stale progress flags or bus traffic do not indefinitely refresh the watchdog, and expiry invokes the declared response within the qualified bound without requiring the stalled work to cooperate</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-010-03">
          <given>an output MCU whose watchdog expires while its output is active</given><when>the response and restart occur</when><then>local triac and relay actuation return to the defined hardware-off states within declared commutation/release bounds, retained electrical faults are not cleared by the restart, and actuation resumes only after valid control and timing are established</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-010-04">
          <given>a main-ESP watchdog expiry while autonomous output boards remain operating</given><when>the ESP resets or stops refreshing valid commands</when><then>outputs follow their configured communication-failure policy under MOT-AC-007 and any higher-priority inhibits; ESP restart does not bypass fault rearm or generate an unintended actuation command</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-ENG-010-05">
          <given>startup, factory-loader or recovery mode, firmware/storage updates, low-power operation and production debug settings</given><when>watchdog behavior is qualified</when><then>startup and intentional maintenance have explicit bounded policies with appropriate output inhibition, loader-managed refresh and legitimate long operations are accounted for, and maintenance/debug exceptions do not silently disable required production supervision</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-STOR-001" type="CR" title="Verified double-buffered persistence on every MCU" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Updates to mutable persistent state on every Motley MCU, including state managed by dependencies or SDKs, SHALL use at least two persistent copies, preserve the currently committed copy while preparing another, and verify the newly stored copy before durably changing the active selector.</statement>
      <notes>This governs how state selected for persistence is stored; it does not decide which commands or measurements become persistent. The scope includes the main ESP, any output-module MCU and other future MCUs, with an inventory of application, SDK, boot/update and reset/migration storage paths. The active selector may be represented by protected commit metadata rather than a literal pointer. Native NVS, journaling, redundant records or API success are not by themselves evidence of conformance. Current Matter/Thread persistence is delegated to SDK storage and remains unassessed against this requirement; no firmware implementation or qualified storage layout is claimed.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-STOR-001-01">
          <given>every MCU's writable persistent-storage inventory and storage layout</given><when>an update erases or writes an inactive copy</when><then>the committed copy and its recoverable activation metadata remain intact in independently erasable storage regions or an explicitly qualified equivalent failure domain; two addresses that are lost in the same erase operation do not establish double buffering</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-STOR-001-02">
          <given>a proposed new persistent transaction</given><when>its inactive copy has been written and durability operations have completed</when><then>verification reads the stored bytes with documented cache/coherency guarantees and checks the complete payload and required length, generation, schema and integrity metadata before activation; an API success, RAM-only checksum or cached copy of the write buffer is insufficient</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-STOR-001-03">
          <given>a failed erase, write, readback or verification before activation</given><when>the update result is handled</when><then>the new copy is not selected, the previous committed state remains usable, and the failure is reported rather than acknowledged as persisted</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-STOR-001-04">
          <given>reset or power loss injected during inactive-copy erase/write, verification, selector update or subsequent reclamation</given><when>the MCU recovers</when><then>it selects a complete committed old or new transaction according to the durable activation state, never mixed data or a merely verified but uncommitted candidate; torn or corrupt selector metadata does not destroy recoverability of the prior commit</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-STOR-001-05">
          <given>settings or records whose consistency depends on one another</given><when>they are updated, migrated, deleted or intentionally reset</when><then>they are committed as one recoverable logical transaction, rather than independently activating incompatible generations; recovery respects a committed deletion/reset instead of resurrecting its superseded state</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-STOR-001-06">
          <given>startup with corrupt, incomplete or unsupported stored data or commit metadata</given><when>persistent state is restored</when><then>only a valid committed compatible copy is used; if none is usable, storage unavailability is reported and applicable defined safe defaults apply without silently formatting storage, activating an uncommitted candidate or treating arbitrary bytes as configuration</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-STOR-001-07">
          <given>a request to acknowledge an update as persistent</given><when>success is reported to its caller or controller</when><then>both the data verification and durable, verified activation commit have completed, and a subsequent ordinary restart restores that committed transaction</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-STOR-001-08">
          <given>a persistent-storage backend or dependency claimed to satisfy this requirement</given><when>its verification evidence is reviewed</when><then>the affected MCU, storage geometry, readback/durability contract, transaction and selector protocol, failure-injection cases and observed recovery results are identified, including SDK-owned paths; host simulations alone do not establish real flash power-loss behavior</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-SENS-005" type="FR" title="Sensor power readiness" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Before communicating with a supported sensor, Motley SHALL enable any required board-controlled sensor power and wait until the sensor's documented supply and startup conditions are met.</statement>
      <notes>The earlier Adafruit ESP32-C6 Feather #5933 direction used active-high GPIO20 for its STEMMA QT supply (https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-esp32-c6-feather/pinouts). The integrated-controller study supersedes that mapping: active-high GPIO11 enables its separate AP2112 sensor regulator, with a receiver-local 10k pull-down. Future board initialization must use the selected hardware revision's mapping. Sensor-bus off-domain/reset-pull behavior and startup qualification remain open; no fixed startup delay or sensing firmware implementation has been selected.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-SENS-005-01">
          <given>a supported sensor on a board-controlled power rail that is initially disabled</given>
          <when>sensing is initialized</when>
          <then>the rail is enabled and the sensor's documented supply and startup conditions are met before the first bus transaction to that sensor</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-001" type="PR" title="Complete pin bias and state review" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Every hardware revision SHALL have a documented assessment of the need for pull-up, pull-down or other state-defining measures for every component pin and connector, switch or jumper contact.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-001-01">
          <given>the schematic, component pin definitions and supported assembly variants of a hardware revision</given>
          <when>its pin review is reconciled with those sources</when>
          <then>every reference and pin or contact has a traceable disposition, including unused pins, unpopulated parts, connector and jumper contacts; equivalent passive pins and repeated circuits may share an explicit assessment without leaving pins unaccounted for</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-001-02">
          <given>a pin or contact whose voltage or current can affect circuit behavior</given>
          <when>its state-defining measures are assessed</when>
          <then>the assessment identifies its electrical function, required states and relevant driver states during normal operation, startup, reset, bootloader operation, brownout, shutdown, partial power, high impedance and removable-connection states, using manufacturer evidence and recording unresolved assumptions</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-001-03">
          <given>a proposed external bias or an explicit decision to omit one</given>
          <when>the disposition is reviewed</when>
          <then>it records the existing internal or external state-defining path, its power domain and physical location, whether the path survives disconnection, and applicable threshold, leakage, internal-pull, loading, tolerance, transient-coupling and unpowered-injection limits; a justified no-additional-bias disposition is permitted and unknown limits remain open findings</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-001-04">
          <given>a claimed closed pin-review finding</given>
          <when>its evidence is inspected</when>
          <then>the source revision, required state, relevant worst-case calculation or measurement and applicable limits are recorded; typical values, schematic connectivity and a clean ERC alone do not establish a guaranteed default or noise margin</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-002" type="CR" title="Defined hardware defaults without active control" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Hardware state-sensitive nets SHALL attain their declared default states without application-firmware initialization whenever their intended drivers are high impedance or absent in the supported power, reset and removable-connection states.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-002-01">
          <given>heater and cutoff controls whose MCU is unpowered, resetting, starting, in the bootloader or otherwise high impedance</given>
          <when>the supported supply sequences and voltage limits are exercised</when>
          <then>opto-triac LED and relay-coil drive remain within declared non-actuation current and pulse-duration limits, allowing declared triac commutation and relay-release times after a previously active command; an explicit intentional command is needed to energize either actuator</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-002-02">
          <given>a receiving board or circuit with a removable low-voltage connector, switch or jumper in its control path</given>
          <when>each supported unplugged, open, missing-shunt and configuration state is evaluated</when>
          <then>the required default is established by paths that remain with the receiving circuit; a bias path available only through the removed item is not credited, and intermediate contact-loss or connection-order cases are either qualified or explicitly excluded from the supported connection procedure</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-002-03">
          <given>power-enable, source-selection, reset, boot-configuration and interface-control nets</given>
          <when>their drivers are high impedance or absent in each applicable state</when>
          <then>their declared defaults account for internal pulls and reset behavior; the sensor supply defaults disabled, source selection follows the declared priority, and USB data attachment remains disconnected without valid host VBUS</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-002-04">
          <given>a required default across powered and unpowered domains</given>
          <when>bias strength and the declared disturbance envelope are checked over the supported tolerances, temperatures and supply states</when>
          <then>receiver thresholds and unpowered-pin or injection limits are respected without excessive driver loading, unintended back-power or an unsafe control pulse; additional filtering or isolation is used where a resistor alone cannot meet those limits</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-003" type="PR" title="Traceable native hardware design" status="approved" priority="must">
      <statement>Native hardware design sources SHALL identify their applicable requirements through explicit RQML file relationships and requirement fields on relevant schematic symbols or sheets and corresponding PCB footprints.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-003-01">
          <given>the native schematic hierarchy and PCB designs</given><when>their traceability is checked</when><then>all annotated native files have explicit RQML relationships; included sheets are covered individually rather than inferred from the root, and applicable requirement identifiers exist in the governing specification</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-003-02">
          <given>schematic symbols represented by PCB footprints, including repeated sheets and multi-unit parts</given><when>their requirement fields are reconciled</when><then>the fields agree using stable source UUID and hierarchical instance identity, with no dependency on reference designators remaining unchanged</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-003-03">
          <given>the traceability report and RQML links</given><when>they are reviewed</when><then>design-input applicability, implementation coverage and verification evidence remain distinguishable; annotation or file-drift checks do not claim circuit correctness, native KiCad navigation or interpretation of component fragments by RQML</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-004" type="CR" title="External-interface ESD assessment" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Every externally accessible electrical interface SHALL have a documented ESD assessment and appropriate protective measures for its declared exposure and operating states.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-004-01">
          <given>external and service connectors, including mains and unused contacts</given><when>the interface inventory is reviewed</when><then>each contact has a protection or justified no-additional-device disposition covering signal/power domain, normal voltage, powered-off behavior, capacitance/leakage, discharge return and enclosure or shield assumptions</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-004-02">
          <given>proposed ESD devices and their placement</given><when>their integration is checked</when><then>protection preserves isolation, PE continuity and required default states, does not create unintended partial-contact or back-power paths, and records residual clamp, layout and return-path limitations</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-004-03">
          <given>a claim of system ESD immunity</given><when>it is accepted</when><then>the exposure levels, setup, operating states, pass criteria and observed tests are recorded; component ratings or ERC alone are not accepted as system immunity evidence</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-005" type="IR" title="Board handling touch reference" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Each PCB design SHALL provide a clearly marked finger-contact point on its top/front side for equalizing potential with its local low-voltage ground during supported handling.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-005-01">
          <given>each complete or supported trimmed board</given><when>its handling point is inspected</when><then>an exposed ground contact on the board's top/front side remains accessible in the low-voltage region, clear of mains and required antenna or insulation reserves, and its ground identity is documented</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-005-02">
          <given>an isolated or floating low-voltage ground</given><when>the handling feature is described or qualified</when><then>local potential equalization is distinguished from earth discharge; no PE-to-logic-ground bond or safe energized-mains handling claim is introduced by the feature</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-006" type="IR" title="Low-cost clip probe access" status="draft" priority="should">
      <statement>Each PCB design SHOULD provide documented clip-compatible probe access to local low-voltage ground, supply rails and selected diagnostic signals with a stated incremental cost.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-006-01">
          <given>the proposed probe-point set</given><when>its implementation is reviewed</when><then>net names, clip or optional loop attachment, polarity/ground references and fitted versus bare-PCB cost are explicit, and retained-board points survive supported trimming</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-006-02">
          <given>probe-point placement in a mixed-voltage design</given><when>clearance and access are assessed</when><then>added clip points remain on the low-voltage side with room for the intended clip; no mains clip point or permission to defeat isolation with grounded test equipment is implied</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-007" type="CR" title="Preferred PCB outline envelope" status="draft" priority="should">
      <statement>Each individual PCB design SHOULD have an overall outline width and height no greater than 100 mm.</statement>
      <rationale>The developer set 100 mm as a strong per-board target while retaining the option to agree a larger board. This applies to the full untrimmed outline, including the sensor board's common circuitry; it does not fix that circuitry's aspect ratio or the size of a manufacturing panel.</rationale>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-007-01">
          <given>an individual PCB outline, including any L-shaped or other nonrectangular shape</given><when>its overall dimensions are reviewed</when><then>the maximum extents along the board's defined width and height axes are compared with 100 mm; exactly 100 mm meets the target, and exceeding it is a deviation to discuss under MOT-HW-008 rather than an absolute prohibition</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-008" type="PR" title="Discuss anticipated PCB size overruns" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>When either overall outline dimension of an individual PCB is expected to exceed 100 mm, the proposed size and relevant tradeoffs SHALL be discussed with the developer before proceeding with the enlargement.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-008-01">
          <given>placement, connector, routing, thermal or other design work indicating that a board may need more than 100 mm in either dimension</given><when>the potential overrun is recognized</when><then>it is raised for discussion with the developer at that point, with the expected dimensions, reasons and practical alternatives, rather than deferred until the oversized design is complete</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-008-02">
          <given>a proposed dimension greater than 100 mm</given><when>the discussion concludes</when><then>the agreed size decision is recorded; a larger board may proceed when agreed, and the 100 mm target remains applicable to other boards</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-009" type="IR" title="Separate Qwiic access to both controller I2C ports" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>The controller board SHALL expose each of its two hardware I2C controllers through a pair of parallel four-contact 3.3 V Qwiic-compatible connectors for flexible peripheral and bench use.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-009-01">
          <given>the controller's general-purpose and low-power I2C controllers</given><when>their connector pin mappings are reviewed</when><then>each has two sockets with contacts 1 GND, 2 3.3 V, 3 SDA and 4 SCL, with the bus identity and supported power source documented; duplicates within a pair share that bus, while the two controllers remain distinct buses</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-009-02">
          <given>any socket used without a multiplexer board</given><when>its bus is powered and configured</when><then>appropriate local pull-ups and connector protection are present, and disconnected, unpowered and externally powered cases have explicit supported-use limits</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-010" type="IR" title="Optional mux Qwiic upstream bench input" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>The multiplexer board SHALL provide two parallel four-contact 3.3 V Qwiic-compatible upstream connectors and two parallel five-contact deployment connectors for chaining, retaining the deployment reset signal.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-010-01">
          <given>the complete or any supported trimmed multiplexer board</given><when>its upstream interfaces are inspected</when><then>both Qwiic connectors expose GND, 3.3 V, SDA and SCL in standard order; both five-contact connectors retain those signals and shared mux reset, and every upstream connector remains in the retained common area</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-010-02">
          <given>standalone bench operation through Qwiic</given><when>the supported connection procedure is followed</when><then>the mux has configurable local bus pull-ups and retained reset bias, deployment connections to another controlling host or supply are disconnected, and one supply and one controlling host serve the bus; optional power-link changes are not represented as signal isolation or automatic source selection</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-011" type="IR" title="Switch-selectable mux address" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Each multiplexer board SHALL provide three address switches selecting its TCA9548A address from 0x70 through 0x77.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-011-01">
          <given>the powered mux board with address switches configured before operation</given><when>the switch truth table is reviewed</when><then>OFF defines zero and ON defines one for A0, A1 and A2, with address 0x70 plus their binary value; all switches OFF selects 0x70, and physical switch positions and address weights are documented</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-011-02">
          <given>open address-switch contacts on a complete or supported trimmed board</given><when>address-pin state-defining paths are inspected</when><then>each address input has a local pull-down that survives cable removal and supported trimming; no address pin relies on an open switch or another board for its defined LOW state</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-HW-012" type="IR" title="Disconnectable mux upstream pull-ups" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Each multiplexer board SHALL permit its upstream SDA and SCL pull-ups to be independently disconnected for shared-bus configurations.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-HW-012-01">
          <given>multiple boards sharing an upstream bus</given><when>the documented pull-up configuration is applied</when><then>either board-local pull-up can be removed from its signal without coupling SDA to SCL through a shared floating resistor supply, changing reset bias or removing required upstream connector signals</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-SENS-006" type="FR" title="Exclusive sensor visibility across multiplexers" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>The sensor-bus scheduler SHALL maintain at most one connected downstream channel across all multiplexers on a shared upstream bus while accessing sensors whose I2C addresses conflict.</statement>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-SENS-006-01">
          <given>a transition between sensors on different addressed multiplexers</given><when>the next sensor is accessed</when><then>the previously selected channel is deselected before enabling the next, rather than assuming that writing a different multiplexer clears the previous device's selection</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-SENS-006-02">
          <given>startup, reset or recovery with unknown multiplexer selection state</given><when>sensor access resumes</when><then>the scheduler establishes a known deselected state on the relevant bus before selecting a sensor; this does not imply that a previously started sensor-heater pulse has stopped</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-001" type="FR" title="Leading-edge phase-angle control" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Each AC output SHALL support configurable leading-edge phase-angle control synchronized to the AC line for qualified compatible loads.</statement>
      <notes>Firing resolution, timing error, command-to-power mapping, supported load types and minimum controllable load remain design parameters to settle before implementation. This does not select central timing, a local MCU or a dedicated firing controller.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-001-01">
          <given>a qualified load and supported mains conditions</given><when>off, full-on and intermediate phase-angle commands are exercised</when><then>observed positive and negative half-cycle firing follows the configured command within declared timing tolerances, without an unintended persistent polarity imbalance</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-001-02">
          <given>startup, a mode change or a missing or invalid line-timing reference</given><when>phase-angle operation is requested</when><then>firing remains inhibited until timing is qualified; a later loss of valid timing stops firing within the declared timeout</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-002" type="FR" title="Whole-cycle heater modulation" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Each AC output SHALL support configurable time-proportional heater control using zero-cross-aligned bursts of complete AC cycles.</statement>
      <notes>Modulation periods, duty resolution and permissible switching error remain open parameters. Whole-cycle scheduling is distinct from free-running PWM and from phase-angle control.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-002-01">
          <given>a qualified resistive heater and a configured modulation period</given><when>zero, full and intermediate duty commands are exercised</when><then>observed delivery follows the commanded complete-cycle allocation within declared rounding and timing limits, including matched positive and negative half-cycles</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-002-02">
          <given>an operating output</given><when>its modulation mode or duty command changes</when><then>the transition follows a declared cycle-boundary policy without an unintended on pulse or persistent half-cycle imbalance</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-002-03">
          <given>startup, a mode change or a missing or invalid line-timing reference</given><when>whole-cycle burst operation is requested</when><then>firing remains inhibited until timing is qualified; a later loss of valid timing stops firing within the declared timeout</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-003" type="FR" title="Per-output shorted-triac identification without a load dependency" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Motley SHALL identify the affected output when its triac has failed short circuit during a gate-off diagnostic interval, both with a supported load attached and with no external load attached.</statement>
      <notes>A simulated main-terminal short establishes the required failure coverage; the reported diagnosis can be unexpected energization consistent with a shorted triac because sensing alone may not uniquely distinguish all wiring or driver faults. Observation requires an available mains source and a conductive path to the triac. Continuous identification behind an open cutoff is not implied. A healthy unloaded triac need not latch, so absence of normal no-load firing is not by itself a failure. Detection during full-on or near-full-on operation without a sufficiently long natural off interval is deferred. Inserting diagnostic off intervals remains an optional future software policy, subject to the sensing circuit's timing limits.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-003-01">
          <given>available mains at the triac and a simulated short across one output triac's main terminals</given><when>gate drive is disabled and the declared observation interval elapses</when><then>the affected output is reported faulty in separate tests with no external load, the minimum supported load and the maximum supported load; a healthy neighboring output is not attributed the fault</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-003-02">
          <given>a healthy gate-off output with each supported snubber configuration and no external load</given><when>leakage, residual charge, supply variation, tolerances and switching transients are exercised over the declared operating envelope</when><then>the shorted-triac diagnosis is not asserted solely from permitted leakage or transient detector activity</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-003-03">
          <given>missing mains, an open upstream cutoff, unavailable feedback or no qualified gate-off observation since startup</given><when>triac diagnostic status is reported</when><then>the current observation is unavailable or not yet tested rather than healthy, while any earlier fault and its output attribution remain reported; restoring observation does not automatically clear the fault or authorize re-energization solely to repeat a test</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-003-04">
          <given>the proposed detection circuit and normal modulation behavior</given><when>the design is qualified</when><then>numeric detection thresholds, hysteresis or debounce, minimum usable gate-off observation duration and maximum detection latency measured from the start of a qualifying off interval are recorded and tested; full-on and near-full-on conditions without a qualifying interval are reported as currently unobservable rather than healthy, without requiring inserted diagnostic off intervals</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-004" type="FR" title="Measured delivery agrees with output commands" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Motley SHALL compare independently observed per-output electrical delivery with the commanded conduction pattern and report mismatches within declared detection limits.</statement>
      <notes>The current full-feature proposal combines per-output voltage-presence feedback with current observations under MOT-AC-010; the final detector and sensor circuits remain open. Accurate RMS voltage, real watts, energy and nominal-voltage power estimates remain optional proposals, not required metering features. Shared line timing or voltage measurement is compatible with this requirement; a separate voltage ADC per output is not prescribed.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-004-01">
          <given>a connected qualified load within the declared detectable range</given><when>missing firing, firing at the wrong phase or unintended delivery in a commanded-off interval is injected in either modulation mode</when><then>the affected output and mismatch are reported within declared latency and timing thresholds; an averaged duty value or a gate command echo alone is not accepted as evidence of correct conduction timing</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-004-02">
          <given>an unplugged load, an open load, a load below the detectable range or unavailable or stale observations</given><when>delivery status is evaluated</when><then>the result distinguishes absent or unverified delivery from a proven failed triac, and missing observations are not presented as successful operation or proof of a de-energized outlet</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-005" type="FR" title="Cutoff and retained diagnosis after unintended delivery" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Detection of unintended output energization SHALL inhibit firing and command a second cutoff actuator to interrupt that output's supply, retaining the fault until an explicit qualified rearm.</statement>
      <notes>This draft proposes latched handling for review. Per-output versus shared cutoff, interruption latency, fault-retention behavior across a power cycle and rearm conditions remain open. A second actuator is not by itself an independent controller or protection temperature sensor.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-005-01">
          <given>a detected shorted-triac or other unintended-delivery fault</given><when>fault handling executes</when><then>gate drive is inhibited and the cutoff is commanded open within the declared reaction time; with a functional cutoff, delivered power is interrupted within the declared total time even though the triac remains shorted</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-005-02">
          <given>a fault followed by cutoff opening, bus reconnection or restored feedback</given><when>ordinary operation is requested</when><then>the original output identity and fault remain available, loss of voltage caused by cutoff is not treated as triac recovery, and an ordinary power command does not bypass the qualified rearm procedure</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-006" type="FR" title="Device-local thermal regulation" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Thermal regulation and output-fault handling SHALL execute within the device without depending on Home Assistant, a cloud service or an available external network.</statement>
      <notes>Loop ownership between a main controller and optional intelligent output modules remains open. Sensor-loss policy, setpoint persistence and thermal protection beyond electrical output-fault handling still need separate definition.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-006-01">
          <given>valid local sensor input and an established control configuration</given><when>external network and supervisory services become unavailable</when><then>local regulation and electrical fault detection/cutoff continue according to that configuration without external timing or control decisions</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-007" type="FR" title="Configurable output response to communication failure" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>An autonomous output module receiving actuation commands from another controller SHALL apply its per-output configured communication-failure response when command validity expires, supporting off, on, hold latest and hold set.</statement>
      <notes>This is conditional on selecting an autonomous module architecture; it does not require a bus or an additional MCU. Off means zero output with cutoff requested; on means full output; hold latest retains the last valid commanded modulation mode and level; hold set uses a separately configured fallback mode and level. Holding a level continues its normal modulation rather than freezing an instantaneous gate or relay state. These are runtime responses of a powered, operating module and do not override hardware default-off states, invalid line-timing inhibition or retained electrical faults. Bus type, refresh/expiry limits, configuration persistence and cutoff ownership remain design questions.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-007-01">
          <given>a powered module executing a valid command with a configured communication-failure response</given><when>its command source stalls, disconnects or sends invalid traffic without a valid refresh</when><then>within the declared expiry deadline the output transitions to zero with cutoff requested for off, full output for on, the last valid commanded mode and level for hold latest, or the separately configured fallback mode and level for hold set, subject to higher-priority inhibits; invalid traffic does not refresh validity</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-007-02">
          <given>startup or reset before any valid actuation command, or runtime operation under a communication-failure response</given><when>communication becomes available</when><then>startup/reset output remains off until a valid actuation command is received, and runtime fallback is replaced only by a fresh valid actuation command rather than mere bus activity, subject to retained faults and rearm conditions</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-007-03">
          <given>independently configured outputs and each supported modulation mode</given><when>response policy, command-expiry interval or hold-set fallback settings are configured</when><then>each output supports all four responses with validated settings; absent or invalid response configuration uses off, and changing one output's response does not change another output's response</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-007-04">
          <given>any communication-failure response, including on or either hold response</given><when>an electrical fault or invalid line-timing condition requires inhibition</when><then>the applicable firing inhibition and cutoff/rearm behavior take precedence over fallback; the cutoff remains operable with a stalled command bus, and selecting or entering fallback does not clear a retained fault</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-007-05">
          <given>a module whose command validity has expired</given><when>its diagnostic state is inspected through an available interface</when><then>the module identifies communication failure, the selected response, the effective fallback mode and level, and any higher-priority inhibit rather than representing fallback as a fresh controller command</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-007-06">
          <given>a communication-failure policy that changes the requested output, including on following a zero normal command</given><when>fallback becomes active</when><then>conduction feedback is compared against the effective local target after fallback and higher-priority inhibits are applied; delivery matching that target does not itself trigger an unintended-energization diagnosis, while mismatches remain subject to MOT-AC-004</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-008" type="CR" title="Isolated AC sensing and control boundary" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>AC sensing and control SHALL preserve the declared mains-to-accessible-low-voltage isolation boundary across all supported power and external-interface configurations.</statement>
      <notes>This includes USB, Qwiic, debugging, probe access and any output-control bus. Isolated current sensing alone does not qualify a resistor-based voltage channel or its supply/data paths. Required insulation classification, working voltage, surge envelope and spacing remain to be selected for the product before circuit qualification.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-008-01">
          <given>all mains-connected sensing, drive, supply and communication paths</given><when>the isolation design and supported connected/unpowered states are reviewed and qualified</when><then>every crossing has documented component ratings and PCB spacing meeting the selected insulation and working-voltage criteria; connecting grounded external equipment does not bypass the boundary</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-009" type="NFR" title="Preferred output-board capacity" status="draft" priority="should">
      <statement>One Motley controller SHOULD support at least 16 independently addressable single-output boards operating concurrently on one shared output-control bus within the declared wiring, power-distribution and communication envelope.</statement>
      <notes>Sixteen is a preferred capacity, not a hard minimum or maximum, and the initial device still uses two output boards. A lower capacity may be agreed when sixteen adds significant cost or difficulty; greater capacity is desirable when inexpensive and straightforward. This does not increase the current rating of the mains input, distribution or controller supply. Bus selection, addressing, topology, wiring length, traffic rates and supply capacity remain design parameters to qualify. The criteria below test the declared supported count; an address-space calculation alone does not establish that count.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-009-01">
          <given>the declared supported count of single-output boards on one output-control bus</given><when>their addresses are assigned and commands and status reads target each board</when><then>every board can be selected independently without address collisions or unintended changes to other boards, using the same supported board hardware and firmware with the documented address configuration</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-009-02">
          <given>the declared supported count of boards operating under the declared maximum normal command/status workload, including supported simultaneous modulation modes</given><when>the controller services the bus and refreshes command validity</when><then>the declared command, status and fault-reporting latency limits are met without unintended command expiry, and local firing and feedback continue to meet their timing limits</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-009-03">
          <given>the proposed supported wiring and power arrangement populated with the declared supported count of boards</given><when>electrical and timing qualification is performed</when><then>bus loading and rise/LOW margins, supply voltage/current limits and line-reference distribution are qualified for the populated arrangement; available address space alone is not accepted as proof of operation at that count</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-009-04">
          <given>a proposed output-bus architecture or capacity below sixteen</given><when>the supported count is selected</when><then>the cost and complexity of reaching sixteen and of inexpensive further expansion are recorded; any reduction below sixteen is discussed and agreed with the developer before being adopted, and the selected count is stated explicitly</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-010" type="FR" title="Per-output current observation referenced to line timing" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Each autonomous output board SHALL provide time-resolved load-current observations to its local MCU, preserving mains isolation and permitting correlation of current conduction with the shared line-timing reference.</statement>
      <notes>The developer restored current sensing to the full-feature proposal before considering later simplification. An isolated Hall sensor plus MCU ADC and a digital current-sensing device remain alternatives. Voltage-presence feedback remains necessary for the unloaded shorted-triac case. Current phase observation does not establish general inductive-load compatibility or a real-power measurement; supported load behavior, measurement limits and processing/reporting interfaces remain to be qualified.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-010-01">
          <given>a qualified load and supported modulation mode</given><when>load current is observed alongside the line reference and gate commands</when><then>the local MCU has observations with declared current range, minimum detectable signal, noise/offset limits, bandwidth and timing uncertainty sufficient to distinguish the supported current-conduction intervals; a slow RMS or averaged value alone does not establish current-phase observation</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-010-02">
          <given>representative resistive and inductive test cases within the declared safe qualification envelope</given><when>current timing and polarity are compared with an independent measurement</when><then>the declared measurement tolerances are met, including supported delayed current zero crossings and phase-cut waveforms; an ambiguous or below-range observation is identified as such rather than reported as a reliable phase value</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-010-03">
          <given>an absent or open load, permitted snubber/detector leakage and the declared lamp/load inrush envelope</given><when>the current-sensing path is evaluated</when><then>its background signal and low-load margins are recorded, its electrical/thermal limits are respected, and any clipping or saturation is accounted for in diagnostic validity and recovery</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-011" type="FR" title="ESP-assisted output-board commissioning and programming" status="draft" priority="should">
      <statement>The main ESP controller SHOULD be able to program and verify each output-board MCU from its factory-unprogrammed state and reprogram it subsequently without requiring an external programmer or a preinstalled custom output application or bootloader.</statement>
      <notes>This is the expected capability unless significant cost or difficulty justifies an agreed alternative. A factory ROM loader or ESP-driven debug interface may satisfy it. Initial commissioning permits one new board responding at the selected default address at a time; already commissioned boards and other boards at nonconflicting addresses may remain connected to the same bus. The restriction is on responders at the selected default, not the total connected board count or commissioning status alone. The selected mechanism must account for loader addresses, boot options, independent board selection and recovery rather than relying only on working application firmware.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-011-01">
          <given>a factory-unprogrammed output MCU and the documented commissioning connections</given><when>the ESP performs initial commissioning</when><then>it selects the intended board, programs and independently verifies the stored image, and starts the intended application without a separate programmer or previously installed custom code</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-011-02">
          <given>an output board with an erased, corrupt or nonresponsive application, or an interrupted programming attempt</given><when>the documented recovery procedure is followed</when><then>the ESP can regain programming access and verify a valid replacement image without depending on execution of that application; required boot/reset controls, option settings and any service reconnection are documented</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-011-03">
          <given>all supported board populations and programming states</given><when>one board is commissioned, updated or recovered</when><then>only the intended board is written, shared-loader address collisions cannot invalidate target identification or verification, and the affected output remains inhibited until valid application control is established</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-011-04">
          <given>a proposed omission or restriction of ESP-assisted initial or subsequent programming</given><when>the design is selected</when><then>the cost or difficulty, viable alternatives and resulting tooling or service steps are presented to and agreed with the developer before the capability is reduced</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-011-05">
          <given>one new output board responding at the selected commissioning/default address, with other supported boards present on the same bus at nonconflicting addresses</given><when>the new board is first commissioned using the documented procedure</when><then>commissioning succeeds without requiring the other boards to be disconnected, only the intended default-address target is programmed, its complete namespaced hardware identity is read and bound to its assigned runtime address, and the stored image and resulting runtime identity/address are verified; the procedure establishes that only one board responds at the selected default, since a shared-address ACK alone cannot prove that precondition</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-011-06">
          <given>a firmware update or recovery request for a previously identified board, including a board without a working application</given><when>the ESP prepares a target-specific erase or write</when><then>the actual target identity is verified through the selected loader or debug mechanism against the requested board identity before modification; an address match or a shared product-type ID alone is insufficient, and unavailable or mismatched identity prevents a blind target-specific update</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-011-07">
          <given>an output board with a corrupt or nonresponsive application</given><when>the documented recovery selector, such as a marked DIP switch or jumper, is set and reset or power-cycle is applied</when><then>the board enters its independent programming/recovery mechanism without application cooperation, remains inhibited and is accessible to the ESP; selector bias, shared-pin behavior and boot/reset/protection settings are reviewed and verified during initial commissioning, and the supported update procedure preserves recovery access rather than enabling incompatible irreversible locks</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-012" type="IR" title="Global output-board identity and tracking" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>The ESP SHALL identify each output board by a stable globally unique hardware identity and associate its configuration, fault records and firmware-update lifecycle with that identity across restarts, firmware replacement and bus-address changes.</statement>
      <notes>A manufacturer namespace and the complete factory silicon UID, with a documented uniqueness scope, are the preferred identity source without extra parts. Include any device-family or UID-scheme namespace needed to avoid collisions across supported hardware. Replacing the MCU establishes a new hardware identity. This identity is not an authentication secret or runtime address and does not itself implement address discovery. Controller-side identity bindings and persistent records remain subject to MOT-STOR-001.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-012-01">
          <given>each supported output MCU and identity mechanism</given><when>identity support is reviewed</when><then>the namespace, complete identifier encoding, width, documented uniqueness scope and application/loader/debug readout are defined so identities distinguish supported hardware globally; a shared product-type ID, short display hash or bus address is not substituted for the complete board identity</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-012-02">
          <given>multiple output boards with their recorded identities and associated configuration, fault and update records</given><when>boards restart, receive replacement firmware or change runtime bus address, or the ESP restarts</when><then>each board retains its identity and its existing records remain associated with that identity; the ESP revalidates the identity-to-endpoint mapping before applying per-board configuration or updates, and fault records remain attributable when a board is absent</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-012-03">
          <given>a different board appearing at a previously used runtime address, or duplicate, unavailable or conflicting identity observations</given><when>the ESP reconciles connected boards</when><then>it reports the new, missing or ambiguous identity and does not silently transfer another board's configuration, clear its retained faults or redirect its update request; assigning a replacement board to a prior logical role requires an explicit rebind</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-013" type="CR" title="Preferred international mains compatibility" status="draft" priority="should">
      <statement>The controller and AC-output PCB designs SHOULD support international household mains supplies using declared regional assembly and wiring variants rather than requiring separate PCB designs.</statement>
      <notes>The preferred scope for assessment is nominal 100-240 VAC and 50/60 Hz; this is a proposed envelope, not a qualified rating. Actual tolerance bands, installation conditions and regional connectors/protection remain to be selected. The existing 120 VAC, 60 Hz device is the initial use case. International compliance or general inductive-load compatibility is not established by this preference.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-013-01">
          <given>the proposed mains input, output stages, detectors and insulation boundary</given><when>regional support is selected</when><then>supported nominal voltages/frequencies and tolerance limits, load-current limits, component and wiring variants, detector behavior and protection/thermal/spacing assumptions are recorded; extra cost or complexity and exclusions from the preferred scope are reviewed before being adopted</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-013-02">
          <given>each claimed regional configuration and its declared operating limits</given><when>the design is qualified</when><then>source operation, line timing, both modulation modes, feedback and cutoff meet their applicable requirements; connector voltage rating, conductor polarity assumptions and isolation are checked for that configuration rather than inferred from the AC/DC module's input range alone</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-014" type="NFR" title="Initial output-load capability" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>Each AC-output board SHALL support a qualified resistive load of at least 300 W at nominal 120 VAC within its declared operating envelope.</statement>
      <notes>This preserves the initial load target without limiting future capability to 300 W. Greater current capability is desirable when achievable through inexpensive component, cooling or assembly variants; a larger rating is not claimed until the complete path is qualified. Ratings at other voltages, lamp inrush, ambient/enclosure limits, heatsinking and total input capacity remain design parameters. Higher-current feasibility is deferred to https://github.com/altendky/motley/issues/30 and does not block the initial 300 W design; inductive-load support remains deferred.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-014-01">
          <given>a complete output board, supported mains tolerance and declared cooling/enclosure/ambient conditions</given><when>a 300 W nominal-120-V resistive load is operated in the supported modes</when><then>the triac, relay, current sensor, conductors, connectors and protection remain within their declared electrical and thermal limits, including supported startup and fault-interruption cases</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-014-02">
          <given>a proposed higher-current or higher-power configuration</given><when>its support is evaluated</when><then>the limiting components, PCB/cooling changes and incremental cost are recorded; any supported rating is stated by voltage, current and load/thermal conditions and does not uprate the common mains input or distribution</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
    <req id="MOT-AC-015" type="IR" title="Output-bus address allocation and service reservations" status="draft" priority="must">
      <statement>The output-control interface SHALL maintain an explicit address plan that separates assigned runtime endpoints from protocol-reserved, factory-loader, commissioning-default and other conflicting device addresses for every supported topology.</statement>
      <notes>For the proposed 7-bit I2C interface, addresses are recorded without the read/write bit. The STM32C011 candidate's ROM loader uses 0x64; reserve it from normal output-board allocation. Protocol reservations include 0x00-0x07 and 0x78-0x7F. Other supported devices and any application commissioning/discovery defaults require explicit entries with their bus/branch scope. A shared default is not a unique board identity, and a reservation does not establish safe multi-device discovery or commissioning.</notes>
      <acceptance>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-015-01">
          <given>the selected output MCU, bus protocol, service mechanism and supported connected devices</given><when>the address plan is reviewed</when><then>it identifies the runtime allocation pool, excluded values/ranges, each factory and application default, the source and address encoding for each entry, and the bus/branch/topology in which conflicts can occur; unresolved defaults are resolved before the corresponding topology is claimed supported</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-015-02">
          <given>commissioning, readdressing or recovery on a supported bus population</given><when>the ESP assigns or restores runtime addresses</when><then>it excludes reserved/conflicting/in-use endpoints, verifies the assigned address against the intended board identity, stores persistent bindings under MOT-STOR-001, and reports collisions or pool exhaustion without silently reusing another board's identity or endpoint</then>
        </criterion>
        <criterion id="AC-MOT-AC-015-03">
          <given>normal runtime discovery and control traffic, including startup with an unexpected device in a factory-loader mode</given><when>the ESP discovers or addresses output boards</when><then>normal allocation, discovery probes and output commands respect the address plan and do not treat reserved service endpoints as ordinary outputs; loader access occurs only through the documented explicit service procedure</then>
        </criterion>
      </acceptance>
    </req>
  </requirements>
